1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,870
This is an essay from Goodfire about
what is really happening inside a neural

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:05,580
network.

3
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:07,220
Its claim is simple.

4
00:00:07,605 --> 00:00:09,175
The networks are already clean.

5
00:00:10,283 --> 00:00:13,073
For years, researchers
thought neurons were tangled.

6
00:00:13,493 --> 00:00:17,843
One famous neuron seemed to fire for
cat faces and the fronts of cars.

7
00:00:18,233 --> 00:00:20,683
The leading explanation was superposition,

8
00:00:21,133 --> 00:00:24,743
the idea that networks fold
unrelated concepts together.

9
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:27,900
Nick Cammarata went back to the evidence.

10
00:00:28,295 --> 00:00:31,825
Superposition was the wrong
explanation for InceptionV1.

11
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:36,900
Ordinary fixes to the architecture
made the late layers legible,

12
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:40,500
with no change to model
size or training data.

13
00:00:41,517 --> 00:00:42,767
The result is striking.

14
00:00:43,252 --> 00:00:44,722
In the last layer of InceptionV1,

15
00:00:45,952 --> 00:00:50,382
99 to 100 percent of neurons
are easy to interpret.

16
00:00:50,732 --> 00:00:53,982
In early audits of a
32-billion-parameter model,

17
00:00:54,412 --> 00:00:57,822
about 90 percent of
neurons have one main job.

18
00:00:58,883 --> 00:01:01,233
The essay is 65 minutes long,

19
00:01:01,643 --> 00:01:03,473
and it links an interactive microscope.

20
00:01:03,968 --> 00:01:08,118
There you can browse the network
neuron by neuron and judge it yourself.

21
00:01:09,283 --> 00:01:10,813
The message is optimistic.

22
00:01:11,448 --> 00:01:15,198
We can study networks directly,
instead of untangling them first.

